IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ec59-nouns`

-- Row il-59. THE FIVE ITEMS, as typed values.
-- A citation is encoded as TWO descriptions: what the citing words say they point at, and what the target is. A test compares them. Where they differ, the citation does not point at what it says;
-- that is kept in an expected-red file with its count declared in check.sh and encoding.json.
-- A recital is encoded as the two years it prints; a test checks that the earlier index year is the year before the later one.
-- A printed title is encoded as which of two sets of words it carries; that is a record, and no test of it is red.
-- LIMBS. Limb (a) is the question whether the target is as the citing words say; limb (b) is the question whether the citing words still read so in the latest official text of the citing provision.
-- Each limb says where it was read from (see `Where a reading comes from` in ec59-nouns.l4). The Hebrew Wikisource texts are not official.
-- A date for which the sources held give no answer is NOTHING; there is no REFUSE.
-- WHAT THE ROW SAYS ABOUT EACH INSTRUMENT is what the page or the crop prints and what the row records; it does not say that anyone is wrong, and it does not say why the words stand as they do.

